  "enhanced_information": "The presence of bilateral papilledema in this 29-year-old woman suggests increased intracranial pressure, which can be associated with serious underlying conditions such as tumors or other structural abnormalities. The symptoms of intense pulsatile headache, horizontal diplopia, and episodes of monocular amaurosis further support the need for imaging to identify potential causes. Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) of the skull is the most appropriate test as it provides detailed visualization of brain structures and can rule out lesions or abnormalities that may be causing the increased intracranial pressure. This aligns with the consensus that MRI is the preferred imaging modality in cases of suspected intracranial pathology. Other options, such as lumbar puncture or visual evoked potentials, may provide additional information but are not definitive for confirming the diagnosis in this context."
